<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shi Lang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a>–<a href="/years/1696">1696</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shi Lang was a Chinese admiral who served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-zhilong">Zheng Zhilong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> before finally defecting to the Qing in 1651. In 1683 he destroyed the Zheng fleet at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>, leading to the surrender of the Kingdom of Tungning. He then persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> as part of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shi-lang.jpg" alt="Shi Lang" width="282" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Generaal_Shi_Lang_met_een_gezelschap_hoogwaardigheidsbekleders_bij_een_waterput_aan_zee_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shi Lang, also known as Secoe or Sego, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> to a distinguished lineage. He studied military strategy as a youth and became especially skilled in naval warfare. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> later described him as a fierce warrior highly adept in command, though he was also blunt and lacking in courtesy. After minor operations with his father, he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-zhilong">Zheng Zhilong</a>'s fleet as captain of the left vanguard and served in it through most of the early 1640s. After a personal dispute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> killed Shi's father, brother and son, and Shi went over to the Qing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Qing valued his naval experience and his contacts in the main trading ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. In 1656 he accompanied Prince Jidu, a son of Jirgalang, on an expedition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and became Assistant Brigadier-General. In the campaign of 1663 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-family">Zheng family</a> he commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships and men. A plan he submitted in 1668 to drive the Ming remnants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a> was not taken up, and he received a post in the Imperial Bodyguard, attached to the Chinese Bordered Yellow Banner.</p><p>In 1681, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Revolt of the Three Feudatories</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> chose him on the advice of Li Guangdi to lead an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. Shi insisted on a command independent of Yao Qisheng, the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. On 8 July 1683 he sailed from Tongshan with 300 warships and 20,000 soldiers, and on 16 and 17 July he defeated the Zheng commander Liu Guoxuan near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a>. On 3 October he reached Taiwan and received the formal surrender of Liu Guoxuan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-keshuang">Zheng Keshuang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Named General Who Maintains Peace on the Seas and a hereditary marquis, Shi stayed at his post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. He seized almost half the developed land of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> for his own profit, extorted money from local communities and pursued policies meant to isolate the island: the coastal provinces were forbidden to deal with it, and settlers could not bring their families. Though later charged with arrogance, he was received by the emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1688. He died in office in 1696.</p><p>He received the posthumous name Xiangzhuang, and in 1732 his name was entered in the Temple of Eminent Statesmen. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> he is generally denounced as a traitor to the Ming loyalist cause, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> he is remembered as a conqueror and a cruel governor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shi Lang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1683 the Qing admiral <b>Shi Lang</b> destroyed the Zheng fleet at Penghu, and Zheng Keshuang surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jinjiang, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.81,118.58&amp;year=1669" aria-label="Jinjiang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7698 1250 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shi Lang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8148 1520h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8148 1520h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.81,118.58&amp;year=1669">What was here in 1669? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
